spec:
  path: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-16-research-driven-specialise-design.md
  status: approved, unchanged; carried forward from the stale research-driven-specialise
    branch (100+ commits behind main) after review confirmed nothing shipped since
    2026-07-16 duplicates or conflicts with it
  plan: docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-06-research-driven-specialise.md
  plan_status: regenerated against current main this session; the original 2026-07-16
    plan's Task 0 (renaming init/SKILL.md -> specialise/SKILL.md, project init ->
    project specialise) was dropped as obsolete - that rename never happened upstream,
    superseded by the cheaper .claude/commands/specialise.md wrapper already in place

completed:
  - regenerated the implementation plan via superpowers:writing-plans: verified every
    file:line reference against current main, dropped the obsolete Task 0, accounted
    for the wiki's method/product axis adoption since the design was written
  - executed all 6 tasks via superpowers:subagent-driven-development: fresh implementer
    subagent per task, task-scoped spec+quality review after each, ledger tracked
    throughout (.superpowers/sdd/2026-08-06-research-driven-specialise/progress.md)
  - Task 1: `project dep-audit` command (osv-scanner wrapper, Nix-then-container-then-skip
    ladder, wired into the check composite)
  - Task 2: Kubernetes support in `project infra-check` via kubeconform
  - Task 3: four consult/* skills (stack-research, budget-hosting, repo-topology,
    dependency-vetting)
  - Task 4: four specialise/* skills (scaffold-execution, security-scanning, infra-k8s,
    local-sandbox)
  - Task 5: wired the consult branch into .agents/skills/init/SKILL.md's Decide phase
  - Task 6: documentation (AGENTS.md command table, docs/wiki/method/development.md,
    docs/wiki/product/operations.md)
  - Task 2 required one fix round: the original routing (transcribed from the
    2026-07-16 plan) silently skipped IaC validation entirely when Kubernetes files
    also happened to be present and no IaC tool was on PATH - fixed to always
    validate-or-fail regardless of what else is present; independently re-verified by
    the task reviewer across all 9 iac x k8s x tool-availability combinations
  - final whole-branch review (opus) found 7 Important findings across the full
    7912a43..c360671 range; two were escalated to the human as scope/design decisions
    (see decisions below), the other five were clear defects
  - one fix wave (433dccf) closed all 6 code-level findings (finding 7, this file
    itself being stale, is this handoff update); extensive manual verification where
    the sandbox lacked real tools - a real osv-scanner container pull against a
    genuinely vulnerable pinned dependency to prove the FAIL path, fake kustomize/
    kubeconform stand-in binaries to prove the render-then-validate wiring end to end
  - scoped re-review confirmed all 6 findings ADDRESSED, no new Critical/Important
    breakage
  - full test suite (335 tests) and project check/self-test/repo-check green
    throughout every task and every fix round

in_progress: []

next:
  - open a PR for research-driven-specialise-v2; human or lead agent owns merge
  - after merge, delete the SDD workspace
    (.superpowers/sdd/2026-08-06-research-driven-specialise/) and this worktree
  - delete the superseded research-driven-specialise branch (100+ commits stale,
    superseded by this branch; not deleted yet, left for the human to confirm)
  - the design spec's own remaining scope beyond what shipped: consult skills are
    prose-only guidance for a future /specialise session to follow - no session has
    yet run through the actual consultation dialogue end to end on a real project.
    Recommended next real-world validation: run /specialise on a genuinely greenfield
    project and confirm the consult branch actually triggers and routes correctly
  - follow-up (non-blocking, deferred from review): dep-audit's docker-exit-125
    classification doesn't catch every "daemon unreachable" presentation - observed a
    real case (Docker CLI 29.6.2) that returns exit 1 with a message matching none of
    the 7 specified network-failure substrings, so it currently surfaces as
    DEP AUDIT FAILED rather than a skip. Extend the substring list or the exit-code
    check if this bites a real user
  - follow-up (non-blocking, deferred from review): dep-audit no longer streams the
    wrapped tool's own output on a successful run (capturing output was required for
    failure classification) - only the one-line OK banner shows now; restore verbose
    success output if scanner detail-on-success turns out to matter
  - follow-up (non-blocking, deferred from review): has_manifests() in dep-audit does
    up to 14 separate rglob() tree walks instead of one pruned os.walk - fine for the
    manifest-free template today; also currently walks gitignored paths (.venv,
    node_modules), so a developer with local vendored deps could see a false-positive
    "not_applicable" fixture failure. Worth collapsing into one walk that skips
    .git/node_modules/.venv/.worktrees before this runs routinely on real projects
  - the Kubernetes render-then-validate path (kustomize build | kubeconform via
    stdin) is verified for process wiring (fake stand-in binaries proved the exact
    argument construction, stdin content and error propagation) and the stdin CLI
    syntax is corroborated by kubeconform's own documented usage patterns, but is
    NOT verified against the real kustomize/kubeconform binaries end to end - neither
    tool is installed in any sandbox this work ran in. Validate by generating one
    project through specialise/infra-k8s before relying on it for real

decisions:
  - id: research_driven_consultation_design_still_valid
    summary: >-
      the 2026-07-16 approved design for research-driven /specialise consultation
      required no changes; verified against every specialise/* skill shipped since
      (static-analysis, build-pipeline, deployment-pipeline, observability,
      infra-decision) with no overlap or conflict found
  - id: plan_task_0_dropped_not_executed
    summary: >-
      the original plan's orchestrator rename (init -> specialise) never happened
      upstream and was not revived; main's .claude/commands/specialise.md wrapper
      already gives the same /specialise UX more cheaply. This plan targets
      init/SKILL.md as it actually exists
  - id: kubeconform_validates_rendered_kustomize_output
    summary: >-
      when a kustomization is detected, infra-check now runs `kustomize build` and
      pipes the rendered YAML into kubeconform via stdin, rather than pointing
      kubeconform at the raw kustomize source directory (which is partial/patch YAML
      that -strict validation would likely reject). Human-approved fix, not
      end-to-end verified against real binaries - see next.actions
  - id: new_skills_use_the_bare_name_convention
    summary: >-
      all 8 new skills (4 consult/*, 4 specialise/*) use bare name: values and
      catalog keys matching their directory basename (e.g. infra-k8s, not
      specialise-infra-k8s), matching all 58 pre-existing skills with zero exceptions.
      Human-approved rename after the final review flagged the inconsistency;
      directory paths (used by check-repo-contract and init/SKILL.md's wiring) were
      unaffected by the rename
  - id: dep_audit_failure_classification_is_heuristic
    summary: >-
      dep-audit distinguishes "tool/network unavailable" (skip, never a silent pass)
      from "real finding or tool error" (fail) via docker exit code 125 plus a
      7-substring case-insensitive match on captured output, not a fully exhaustive
      classification - see the deferred docker-exit-1 follow-up in next.actions
  - id: handoff_maintenance_is_a_controller_responsibility_in_sdd
    summary: >-
      the final whole-branch review flagged HANDOFF.toon staleness as a finding, but
      it was handled by the controller directly (this update) rather than folded into
      the subagent fix wave - it requires whole-delivery context no single dispatched
      subagent held, and the SDD skill's fix-loop is scoped to code-level findings
      from a diff, not session-level documentation state

risks:
  - the Kubernetes kustomize-render path is unverified against real kustomize/
    kubeconform binaries (see next.actions) - a project relying on
    specialise/infra-k8s before that validation could hit a real, currently-unknown
    integration bug
  - dep-audit's failure classification is a heuristic, not exhaustive (see decisions
    and next.actions) - a docker-daemon-unreachable case on some Docker CLI versions
    will currently surface as a false FAILED rather than a skip
  - no session has run the actual consult/* dialogue end to end against a real
    greenfield project; the skills are well-reviewed prose but unexercised in practice
  - dep-audit is wired into the `check` composite, which the Nix repo-contract
    derivation runs in a network-less sandbox; the fix wave addressed the most likely
    failure mode (network/tool unavailable -> skip, not fail) but this composite
    membership itself was a design choice inherited from the original plan, not
    independently re-litigated this session - worth an explicit decision if it proves
    troublesome for generated projects
  - has_manifests()'s tree walk includes gitignored paths (see next.actions
    follow-up) - low likelihood, not yet hit in practice

